# 3. Stage-by-Stage Circuit Breakdown

## 3.1 High-Side PNP Switches & Level Shifters

### High-Side Transistors

* `Q1` controls **Node A**
* `Q2` controls **Node B**

The emitters of both PNP transistors are connected to the `+11.1 V` supply rail.

### The Voltage Problem

The ESP32 operates with **3.3 V logic**, while the PNP emitters are at approximately `11.1 V`.

Directly connecting an ESP32 GPIO to a PNP base would not provide a proper way to pull the base up to the emitter voltage and turn the transistor OFF.

For example:

```text
PNP emitter = 11.1 V
ESP32 HIGH  = 3.3 V

V_E - V_B = 11.1 - 3.3
          = 7.8 V
```

This would keep the PNP base substantially below its emitter and can force the transistor ON.

### NPN Level-Shifter Solution

Two NPN transistors (`Q5` and `Q6`) are used as level-shifting stages.

```text
ESP32 GPIO 32 ──> Q5 ──> Q1 PNP Base
ESP32 GPIO 33 ──> Q6 ──> Q2 PNP Base
```

#### Q1 / Node A

When **GPIO 32 (HSA)** is HIGH:

```text
GPIO 32 = HIGH
     │
     ▼
    Q5 ON
     │
     ▼
Q1 base pulled LOW
     │
     ▼
Q1 PNP turns ON
     │
     ▼
Node A ≈ +11.1 V
```

When GPIO 32 is LOW:

```text
GPIO 32 = LOW
     │
     ▼
    Q5 OFF
     │
     ▼
Q1 base pulled toward +11.1 V
     │
     ▼
Q1 turns OFF
```

`Q6` provides the equivalent level-shifting function for `Q2` using **GPIO 33 (HSB)**.

